\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
One-Layer ANN in Generalized Fourier Series Expansion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Data Fitting Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Test
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The problem setup is to fit 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset

 given pairs of feature and label (inputs and outputs) 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \left(x_{j},y_{j}\right)\right\} $
\end_inset

 by a function 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\theta}(x)$
\end_inset

 with parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 (usually a vector).
 The parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is found by gradient descent.
 The aim is to study how the parameter changes in the gradient descent algorithm
 by utilizing generalized Fourier series (GFS) expansion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The fitting quality is measured by 2-norm loss 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{L}(\theta)$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{L}_{J}(\theta)=\frac{1}{2J}\sum_{j=1}^{J}\left(f_{\theta}(x_{j})-y_{j}\right)^{2}.\label{eq:Loss-sample}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assume the samples 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ x_{j}\right\} $
\end_inset

 are draw from a distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho(x)$
\end_inset

 (either bounded or unbounded support), and the labels come from an underlying
 function 
\begin_inset Formula $y(x)$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}=y(x_{j})$
\end_inset

.
 Now let 
\begin_inset Formula $J\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

, we should have a theoretical loss 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\theta)$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{L}_{J}(\theta)\rightarrow L(\theta)\quad\mathrm{as}\ J\rightarrow\infty,
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
L(\theta)=\frac{1}{2}\int_{\Omega}\left(f_{\theta}(x)-y(x)\right)^{2}\rho(x)\,\mathrm{d}x.\label{eq:Loss}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:Loss"

\end_inset

 naturally induce a norm and an inner product (assume all real functions)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert f\right\Vert _{\rho}=\int_{\Omega}f^{2}(x)\rho(x)\,\mathrm{d}x,
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle f,g\right\rangle _{\rho}=\int_{\Omega}f(x)g(x)\rho(x)\,\mathrm{d}x.\label{eq:inner-product}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under the inner product in Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:inner-product"

\end_inset

, with possibly a boundary condition, there exist a set of orthogonal basis
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ p_{k}(x)\right\} _{k=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle p_{k},p_{j}\right\rangle _{\rho}=\delta_{kj}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Notably, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k}(x)$
\end_inset

 can be orthogonal (orthonormal) polynomials generated from 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ x^{k}\right\} _{k=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Remarks:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Basis other than polynomials can be obtained from Sturm–Liouville problem,
 such as Bessel function or spherical harmonics (See wikipedia 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_sturmliouville_2018"
literal "true"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
These orthonormal basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ p_{k}\right\} $
\end_inset

 form a Hilbert space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Each 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k}(x)$
\end_inset

 has exactly 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 zeros.
 Therefore the subscript 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 has similar meaning as the 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 in Fourier mode 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{\mathrm{i}kx}$
\end_inset

, i.e.
 the frequency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The fitting function 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\theta}$
\end_inset

 can then be expand as Generalized Fourier series:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sum_{k=0}^{K}c_{k}(\theta)p_{k}(x)\rightarrow f_{\theta}(x)\quad\mathrm{as}\ K\rightarrow\infty\label{eq:orth-expand}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c_{k}(\theta)=\left\langle f_{\theta},p_{k}\right\rangle _{\rho}\label{eq:expand-coef}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\rightarrow$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 means convergence in a suitable sense, which depends on the regularity
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\theta}(x)$
\end_inset

.
 e.g.
 uniformly convergent for Lipschitz continuous 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\theta}(x)$
\end_inset

, point-wise convergent (almost everywhere) for piecewise continuous and
 bounded variation 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\theta}(x)$
\end_inset

, weak convergence for worse cases.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the Hilbert space here, the completeness of basis is measured by convergence
 of norm (
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert \cdot\right\Vert _{\rho}$
\end_inset

).
 See also mathworld.wolfram.com 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "weisstein_complete_nodate"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the following analysis, we assume 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\rightarrow$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is actually 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $=$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: using orthonormal basis other than the one derived above (with the
 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho(x)$
\end_inset

) is possible, in the sense that only use the GFS to look at its frequency
 components.
 For further discussion, see Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Advantages"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Gradient of Loss in View of Generalized Fourier Series
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The expansion of gradient of loss function provides insight for gradient
 descent algorithm:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial L(\theta)}{\partial\theta} & =\frac{1}{2}\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\left\langle f_{\theta}-y,f_{\theta}-y\right\rangle _{\rho}=\frac{1}{2}\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left\langle f_{\theta}-y,p_{k}\right\rangle _{\rho}^{2}=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left\langle f_{\theta}-y,p_{k}\right\rangle _{\rho}\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\left\langle f_{\theta},p_{k}\right\rangle _{\rho}\nonumber \\
 & =\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left(c_{k}(\theta)-\left\langle y,p_{k}\right\rangle _{\rho}\right)\frac{\partial c_{k}(\theta)}{\partial\theta}.\label{eq:Loss-d}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\theta}$
\end_inset

 is 1-layer ANN (
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\{w_{l},r_{l},b_{l}\}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $_{l=1}^{L}$
\end_inset

), with 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in[-1,1]$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f_{\theta}=\sum_{l=1}^{L}w_{l}\sigma(r_{l}x+b_{l}).\label{eq:1-layer-ann}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial f_{\theta}}{\partial\theta}=\begin{cases}
{\displaystyle \sigma(r_{l}x+b_{l})} & \theta=w_{l}\\
w_{l}\sigma'(r_{l}x+b_{l}) & \theta=b_{l}\\
w_{l}\sigma'(r_{l}x+b_{l})x & \theta=r_{l}
\end{cases}\quad l=1,\ldots,L.\label{eq:f-d}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The last term in Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:Loss-d"

\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial c_{k}(\theta)}{\partial\theta}=\left\langle \frac{\mathrm{\partial f_{\theta}}}{\partial\theta},p_{k}\right\rangle _{\rho}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we further assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 is smooth in the sense of Lemma 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "lem:cheb-coef-cn"

\end_inset

 or Lemma 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "lem:cheb-coef-analytical"

\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial f_{\theta}}{\partial\theta}$
\end_inset

 will have similar but possibly slightly degraded smoothness.
 The theorem for general 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho(x)$
\end_inset

 is not yet found, so the following analysis are based on the Chebyshev
 expansion (
\begin_inset Formula $\rho(x)=\left(1-x^{2}\right)^{-1/2}$
\end_inset

).
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho(x)=1$
\end_inset

, it is the Legendre polynomials expansion, it has the same asymptotics
 as Chebyshev expansion (Ref.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "wang_convergence_2012"
literal "true"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Case 1, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma(x)$
\end_inset

 is analytical (in the sense of Lemma 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "lem:cheb-coef-analytical"

\end_inset

), so 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\theta}(x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial f_{\theta}(x)}{\partial\theta}$
\end_inset

 are also analytical.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let's assume that there is no singularity in a neighborhood of real axis
 
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
If there is singularity on the real axis, then it will be non-smooth with
 certain translation parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{l}$
\end_inset

, i.e.
 probably not a very interesting activation function.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 in the domain of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma(x)$
\end_inset

, the nearest singularity is 
\begin_inset Formula $\pm\mathrm{i}B$
\end_inset

 away from the real axis.
 In this setup, the Bernstein ellipse parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 (the 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 in Lemma 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "lem:cheb-coef-analytical"

\end_inset

, not to be confused with the weighting 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho(x)$
\end_inset

) is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta_{\sigma}=B+\sqrt{1+B^{2}}$
\end_inset

 at least.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So the Bernstein ellipse parameter for 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial f_{\theta}}{\partial\theta}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta_{\theta}=B/r_{l}+\sqrt{1+\left(B/r_{l}\right)^{2}}\approx\Theta_{\sigma}/r_{l}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\approx$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{l}\ll B$
\end_inset

), we get
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|\frac{\partial c_{k}(\theta)}{\partial\theta}\right|<2M\Theta_{\theta}^{-k}\sim2M\left(\frac{r_{l}}{\Theta_{\sigma}}\right)^{k},\quad k\geq1.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Case 2, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma(x)$
\end_inset

 is continuous up to 
\begin_inset Formula $(\nu-1)$
\end_inset

-th derivative (in the sense of Lemma 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "lem:cheb-coef-cn"

\end_inset

), so 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\theta}(x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial f_{\theta}(x)}{\partial\theta}$
\end_inset

 are also smooth in the same sense, with 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\nu}=\nu-1$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=b_{l},r_{l}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\nu}=\nu$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=w_{l}$
\end_inset

.
 Due to Lemma 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "lem:cheb-coef-cn"

\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|\frac{\partial c_{k}(\theta)}{\partial\theta}\right|\leq\frac{2V}{\pi(k-\nu)^{\tilde{\nu}+1}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Depending on the curve 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma(x)$
\end_inset

, the total variation 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\mathrm{d}^{\tilde{\nu}}}{\mathrm{d}x^{\tilde{\nu}}}\frac{\partial f_{\theta}}{\partial\theta}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $[-1,1]$
\end_inset

 can have different asymptotic behavior with respect to parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

.
 For Relu 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma(x)=\max(0,x)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu=1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{TV}\left(\sigma^{(\nu)}(r_{l}x)\right)=r_{l}$
\end_inset

, assume the turning point 
\begin_inset Formula $-b_{l}/r_{l}\in[-1,1]$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V=\begin{cases}
r_{l} & \theta=w_{l}\\
w_{l}r_{l} & \theta=b_{l}\\
w_{l}(r_{l}+\max(0,2b_{l})) & \theta=r_{l}
\end{cases}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rest of the analysis is the same as plain Fourier Series based method.
 i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial c_{k}(\theta)}{\partial\theta}$
\end_inset

 act as the weighting to the frequency-wise residuals 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{k}(\theta)-\left\langle y,p_{k}\right\rangle _{\rho}$
\end_inset

 in the gradient descent algorithm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Advantages of Using Data Oriented Generalized Fourier Series
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Advantages"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Summary of advantages (Data Oriented Generalized Fourier Series, DOGFS):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The loss function in ANN is exactly one side of the Parseval's equality
 of DOGFS.
 Thus optimizing loss is equivalent to optimize the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Energy
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (Parseval's).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
DOGFS reveals the intrinsic smoothness of (target and fitting) function,
 in contrast of Fourier series that are contaminated by boundary.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Clear separation of frequency terms in expansion of gradient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Explanation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose we expand 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\theta}$
\end_inset

 by a set of complete orthonormal basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ q_{k}\right\} $
\end_inset

 other than 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ p_{k}\right\} $
\end_inset

 mentioned in Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:orth-expand"

\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f_{\theta}(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}d_{k}(\theta)q_{k}(x),
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left\langle q_{j},q_{k}\right\rangle _{\rho_{q}}=\int_{\Omega}q_{j}(x)q_{k}(x)\rho_{q}(x)=\delta_{jk}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The corresponding 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Plancherel theorem
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Parseval's
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Formula is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{q}(f)=\int_{\Omega}f^{2}(x)\rho_{q}(x)\,\mathrm{d}x=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}d_{k}^{2},
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{q}\neq\rho$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example, using basis of the Fourier series, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{q}\equiv1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{k}(x)=\cos\left(\pi\left\lceil \frac{k}{2}\right\rceil x+\frac{\pi}{2}k\right)$
\end_inset

, assuming the domain 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[-1,1\right]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Clearly, 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}E_{q}(f_{\theta}-y)$
\end_inset

 is different from 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\theta)$
\end_inset

 derived in Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:Loss"

\end_inset

 in general.
 This is an essential requirement that doing gradient descent in frequency
 domain is the same in physical domain.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace defskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Fourier series implicitly imposed a periodic boundary condition: 
\begin_inset Formula $f(-1+0)=f(1-0)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall k\in\mathbb{N},\ f^{(k)}(-1+0)=f^{(k)}(1-0)$
\end_inset

, which the original function 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\theta}(x)$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $y(x)$
\end_inset

 do not usually have.
 Therefore, the high frequency components are trying hard to fit the non-exist
 periodic boundary.
 And that can easily shadows the true smoothness of 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\theta}(x)$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $y(x)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In contrast, in DOGFS, from Lemma 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "lem:cheb-coef-cn"

\end_inset

 or Lemma 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "lem:cheb-coef-analytical"

\end_inset

, we see no suspicious boundary condition is assumed, only the smoothness
 inside the domain (with possibly analytical continuation) is considered.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace defskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now let's see how the gradient goes for 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ q_{k}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial L(\theta)}{\partial\theta} & =\frac{1}{2}\int_{\Omega}\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\left(f_{\theta}(x)-y(x)\right)^{2}\rho(x)\,\mathrm{d}x=\frac{1}{2}\int_{\Omega}\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\left(\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}d_{k}(\theta)q_{k}(x)-y(x)\right)^{2}\rho(x)\,\mathrm{d}x\nonumber \\
 & =\int_{\Omega}\left(\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}d_{k}(\theta)q_{k}(x)-y(x)\right)\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\left(\sum_{k'=0}^{\infty}d_{k'}(\theta)q_{k'}(x)\right)\rho(x)\,\mathrm{d}x\nonumber \\
 & =\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\sum_{k'=0}^{\infty}d_{k}(\theta)\left\langle q_{k},q_{k'}\right\rangle _{\rho}\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}d_{k'}(\theta)-\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left\langle q_{k},y\right\rangle _{\rho}\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}d_{k}(\theta)\nonumber \\
 & =\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left(\sum_{k'=0}^{\infty}d_{k}(\theta)\left\langle q_{k},q_{k'}\right\rangle _{\rho}\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}d_{k'}(\theta)-\left\langle q_{k},y\right\rangle _{\rho}\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}d_{k}(\theta)\right).\label{eq:grad-L-q}
\end{align}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{k}$
\end_inset

 is not basis in DOGFS, now 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle q_{k},q_{k'}\right\rangle _{\rho}\neq\delta_{kk'}$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-th 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Frequency
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 component 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle q_{k},y\right\rangle _{\rho}$
\end_inset

 is in effect with all 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{k}(\theta)$
\end_inset

, which are the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

frequency
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 components of 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 i.e.
 The concept of frequency domain weighted fitting broke.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One might argue that:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It is possible that the concept of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

frequency
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ q_{k}\right\} $
\end_inset

 is not that different from 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ p_{k}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 Probably true in some case, but obviously, the analysis will become more
 complex.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Why not keep use the inner product 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle \cdot,\cdot\right\rangle _{\rho_{q}}$
\end_inset

 in Eq.
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:grad-L-q"

\end_inset

? The answer is, you gain nothing:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial L(\theta)}{\partial\theta} & =\frac{1}{2}\int_{\Omega}\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\left(\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}d_{k}(\theta)\sqrt{\frac{\rho(x)}{\rho_{q}(x)}}q_{k}(x)-\sqrt{\frac{\rho(x)}{\rho_{q}(x)}}y(x)\right)^{2}\rho_{q}(x)\,\mathrm{d}x\\
 & =\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\left(\sum_{k'=0}^{\infty}d_{k}(\theta)\left\langle q_{k},\frac{\rho}{\rho_{q}}q_{k'}\right\rangle _{\rho_{q}}\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}d_{k'}(\theta)-\left\langle q_{k},\frac{\rho}{\rho_{q}}y\right\rangle _{\rho_{q}}\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}d_{k}(\theta)\right).
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

